1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,445
Metals deform plastically at stresses far below

2
00:00:03,445 --> 00:00:07,102
the theoretical strength of a perfect crystal. Why?

3
00:00:07,102 --> 00:00:11,491
The answer, proposed in the nineteen thirties, is dislocations.

4
00:00:14,611 --> 00:00:17,120
To shear a perfect crystal, every bond across

5
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,680
the slip plane would have to break at once.

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:21,574
That needs an enormous stress.

7
00:00:21,994 --> 00:00:25,229
A dislocation breaks the bonds one row at a time.

8
00:00:25,229 --> 00:00:30,544
Each step breaks and re forms only one set of bonds, so it needs far less energy.

9
00:00:30,544 --> 00:00:32,855
It is like a caterpillar moving its hump

10
00:00:32,855 --> 00:00:36,321
forward, instead of lifting all its legs at once.

11
00:00:38,241 --> 00:00:41,659
Dislocations make metals weaker than a perfect crystal,

12
00:00:41,659 --> 00:00:44,961
but they are exactly what allows metals to deform.

13
00:00:44,961 --> 00:00:49,363
If dislocations cannot move, plastic deformation does not happen.

14
00:00:53,062 --> 00:00:56,629
The motion of dislocations under stress is called slip.

15
00:00:56,629 --> 00:01:00,655
The plane a dislocation sweeps is the slip plane, its direction is

16
00:01:00,655 --> 00:01:04,567
the slip direction, and together they form a slip system.

17
00:01:04,987 --> 00:01:08,447
Slip happens on the closest packed planes, which are also

18
00:01:08,447 --> 00:01:11,963
the smoothest, along the closest packed directions.

19
00:01:11,963 --> 00:01:16,274
In F C C, that is the one one one, one one zero system: four one

20
00:01:16,274 --> 00:01:21,662
one one planes, each with three one one zero directions. Twelve slip systems.

21
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:26,065
B C C slips along one one one directions, on one one zero

22
00:01:26,065 --> 00:01:30,242
planes and others, for twelve to forty eight systems.

23
00:01:30,242 --> 00:01:33,636
H C P has only three, on its basal plane.

24
00:01:33,636 --> 00:01:39,315
So F C C and B C C metals are ductile, while H C P metals are often brittle.

25
00:01:39,735 --> 00:01:44,038
Dislocations move easily in metals, with non directional bonds.

26
00:01:44,038 --> 00:01:47,701
In covalent ceramics, directional bonds resist them.

27
00:01:47,701 --> 00:01:51,655
In ionic ceramics, slip would push like charges together.

28
00:01:51,655 --> 00:01:53,981
That is why ceramics are brittle.

29
00:01:54,401 --> 00:01:57,238
And deformation multiplies dislocations.

30
00:01:57,238 --> 00:02:00,980
An annealed metal might hold ten cubed millimeters of line per

31
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:05,084
cubic millimeter, about one meter in a sugar sized grain.

32
00:02:05,084 --> 00:02:07,559
Heavy working pushes it toward ten to the

33
00:02:07,559 --> 00:02:10,818
nine or ten: a thousand kilometers or more.

34
00:02:14,618 --> 00:02:18,890
Even when you pull in pure tension, slip is driven by shear.

35
00:02:18,890 --> 00:02:21,537
The shear stress resolved onto a slip system

36
00:02:21,537 --> 00:02:24,726
is tau R, equals sigma cos phi cos lambda.

37
00:02:25,146 --> 00:02:29,125
Phi is the angle between the load and the slip plane normal, and

38
00:02:29,125 --> 00:02:32,869
lambda the angle between the load and the slip direction.

39
00:02:32,869 --> 00:02:37,141
Slip starts when tau R reaches the critical resolved shear stress.

40
00:02:37,141 --> 00:02:41,178
So the crystal yields at tau critical over cos phi cos lambda.

41
00:02:41,598 --> 00:02:45,258
Callister's example: a B C C iron single crystal,

42
00:02:45,258 --> 00:02:49,047
pulled along zero one zero at fifty two megapascals.

43
00:02:49,047 --> 00:02:53,734
For the one one zero plane and the bar one one one direction, cos phi

44
00:02:53,734 --> 00:02:58,293
is one over root two, and cos lambda is one over root three.

45
00:02:58,714 --> 00:03:03,022
So tau R is fifty two times zero point seven zero seven times zero

46
00:03:03,022 --> 00:03:07,461
point five seven seven: twenty one point two megapascals.

47
00:03:07,461 --> 00:03:12,749
If the critical resolved shear stress is thirty, the crystal yields at thirty

48
00:03:12,749 --> 00:03:18,036
over zero point four zero eight: seventy three point five megapascals.

49
00:03:22,535 --> 00:03:24,876
So how do we make a metal stronger?

50
00:03:24,876 --> 00:03:29,167
One principle covers everything: make dislocations harder to move.

51
00:03:29,167 --> 00:03:34,015
Three mechanisms do it: smaller grains, solute atoms, and cold work.

52
00:03:34,435 --> 00:03:36,849
Solid solution strengthening first.

53
00:03:36,849 --> 00:03:39,838
Solute atoms strain the lattice around them, and

54
00:03:39,838 --> 00:03:42,826
those strain fields pin passing dislocations.

55
00:03:42,826 --> 00:03:46,447
The bigger the size misfit, the stronger the effect.

56
00:03:50,147 --> 00:03:52,376
Grain boundaries are barriers.

57
00:03:52,376 --> 00:03:55,509
A dislocation cannot simply cross into a misoriented

58
00:03:55,509 --> 00:03:59,304
grain, so dislocations pile up against the boundary.

59
00:03:59,724 --> 00:04:04,178
Smaller grains mean more boundaries and a higher yield strength.

60
00:04:04,178 --> 00:04:07,753
That is the Hall-Petch equation: sigma y equals sigma

61
00:04:07,753 --> 00:04:11,266
zero plus k y, times d to the minus one half.

62
00:04:11,686 --> 00:04:13,415
Grain refinement is special.

63
00:04:13,415 --> 00:04:17,071
It is one of the few ways to raise strength and toughness together.

64
00:04:20,771 --> 00:04:24,414
Cold work, or strain hardening: deforming a metal at room

65
00:04:24,414 --> 00:04:28,336
temperature makes it stronger and harder, but less ductile.

66
00:04:28,336 --> 00:04:33,324
The deformation multiplies dislocations, and they tangle and block each other.

67
00:04:33,743 --> 00:04:37,583
Percent cold work is the reduction in cross sectional area.

68
00:04:37,583 --> 00:04:41,306
A zero minus A d, over A zero, times one hundred.

69
00:04:41,726 --> 00:04:45,602
For a copper rod drawn from fifteen point two to twelve point two

70
00:04:45,602 --> 00:04:50,134
millimeters in diameter, that is one minus twelve point two squared over

71
00:04:50,134 --> 00:04:54,786
fifteen point two squared: thirty five point six percent cold work.

72
00:04:58,587 --> 00:05:03,439
Annealing reverses cold work in three stages. First, recovery.

73
00:05:03,439 --> 00:05:08,412
Dislocations rearrange and annihilate, internal strain energy is relieved,

74
00:05:08,412 --> 00:05:12,718
and properties like electrical and thermal conductivity recover.

75
00:05:13,138 --> 00:05:18,272
Second, recrystallization. New, strain free, equiaxed grains nucleate and

76
00:05:18,272 --> 00:05:21,062
grow, consuming the cold worked grains.

77
00:05:21,062 --> 00:05:23,964
Strength drops, and ductility comes back.

78
00:05:24,384 --> 00:05:28,143
It happens at the recrystallization temperature, typically between

79
00:05:28,143 --> 00:05:31,688
one third and one half of the absolute melting temperature.

80
00:05:32,108 --> 00:05:33,824
Third, grain growth.

81
00:05:33,824 --> 00:05:38,419
At higher temperature, large grains grow at the expense of small ones to reduce

82
00:05:38,419 --> 00:05:43,567
boundary energy, and the strength drops a little, just as Hall-Petch predicts.

83
00:05:47,668 --> 00:05:49,604
Here is the summary of topic nine.

84
00:05:50,024 --> 00:05:55,098
Dislocations glide one row of bonds at a time, so metals yield at low stress.

85
00:05:55,518 --> 00:05:58,119
Slip uses the densest planes and directions,

86
00:05:58,119 --> 00:06:00,828
and more slip systems means more ductility.

87
00:06:01,248 --> 00:06:04,554
Schmid's law resolves the load onto the slip system.

88
00:06:04,974 --> 00:06:06,930
And we strengthen metals by blocking

89
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:10,245
dislocations, then soften them again by annealing.

90
00:06:10,245 --> 00:06:14,104
That completes all nine topics. Good luck on the midterm.
